 “99% 𝑪𝑷𝑼 𝑼𝒔𝒂𝒈𝒆” 𝒐𝒏 𝑹𝑯𝑬𝑳 – 𝑩𝒖𝒕 𝑰𝒕 𝑾𝒂𝒔𝒏’𝒕 𝑾𝒉𝒂𝒕 𝒀𝒐𝒖 𝑻𝒉𝒊𝒏𝒌

Alert received:
 🚨 CPU usage > 99% for 5+ minutes
 As the on-call SRE for the week, I jumped in, expecting a CPU-heavy workload or a runaway process. But something didn’t feel right.
👀 A quick look told a different story:
🔍 top showed:
High %wa (I/O wait) — not actual CPU crunching.
Tried iotop and pidstat — not installed (typical on minimal RHEL builds).
 So I used:

           ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head -10

→ Found a dnf process in D-state (uninterruptible sleep), mid-package operation.

🛠️ Then came the usual RHEL checks:

df -h → disk usage was fine (~31%) ✅
/var/lib/dnf/ and /var/cache/dnf/ → active lock files, updated metadata 🔒
tail -f /var/log/dnf.log → showed background activity, but not conclusive.

🧠 What was really going on?

➡️ After more digging, I found signs of a backup agent kicking off snapshot operations — or possibly endpoint security software scanning files aggressively. Both can cause massive I/O pressure without using much CPU.
